WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.130
Marvel Studios' Black Panther has brought
King T'Challa and his home country of Wakanda

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:09.370
to life, drawing inspiration from the hundreds
of Black Panther comics.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.549
Virtually every character in the film was
inspired by a comic book counterpart, and

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:17.420
while they might never see the same twists
and turns in the movies that they did in the

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.250
comics, there's a lot more to them than you
might've thought.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.390
Here are the characters from Black Panther
who might just have a little more meaning

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.390
than you realized.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.390
And in case you haven't seen the movie yet,
spoilers ahead!

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.630
Shuri

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:34.190
In the film, Letitia Wright plays Shuri, the
young technologist responsible for the Black

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.320
Panther’s vibranium-powered tech.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.470
In the comics, however, her role wasn’t
limited to fighting alongside T’Challa.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.309
In 2009, she actually replaced him.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:47.540
While Shuri wasn’t introduced until almost
40 years after Black Panther first appeared

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.489
in 1966, her rise to prominence came pretty
quickly.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.719
After losing the Black Panther identity twice,
T’Challa decided that he’d be a lot better

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:57.969
off choosing his own successor.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:01.690
Since Shuri was his closest relative, and
since she’d already proved her valor by

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.890
defending Wakanda against an invasion by Klaw,
he chose to train her.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.920
Sure enough, her ascension as Black Panther
later became necessary.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.690
After T’Challa was injured to the point
of death, Shuri took the Heart Shaped Herb

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.340
and became the new Black Panther and the ruling
Queen of Wakanda.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.170
Whether or not the movies will follow this
storyline remains to be seen, but as we’ve

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.189
already seen her as a capable warrior in battle
and the brains behind the Black Panther’s

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:27.770
tech, we wouldn’t rule it out.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.280
Ulysses Klaue

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.110
Despite being the character who links Black
Panther to the earlier installments of the

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.909
MCU, Ulysses Klaue, as played by Andy Serkis,
isn’t exactly the major villain of the movie.

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:43.390
Instead, he makes his exit about halfway through,
a red herring that works so well because in

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:47.560
the comics, Klaw is one of Black Panther’s
most enduring arch-enemies.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.840
Like the version in the films, comic book
Klaw was an amoral arms dealer with a keen

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.550
interest in vibranium, and even sported the
sonic “arm-cannon” in place of his left

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.550
hand.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.860
The big difference, though, is that his abilities
didn’t stop there.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.979
In a desperate attempt to gain super-powers
that would make him the equal of the Fantastic

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:07.729
Four, Klaw transformed himself into a being
made of living soundwaves.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.430
Klaw began a supervillainous career that saw
him battling against the Fantastic Four, Daredevil,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.690
and Black Panther, forming a hatred that would
see him amassing an army of supervillains

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.980
to invade Wakanda.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.020
The question is whether the movie version
of Klaue will make a return in a form that’s

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.640
closer to his comics counterpart.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.060
As good as Serkis is in the role, and as nice
as it is to see his actual face for once,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.710
you don’t hire the world’s most famous
motion-capture actor if you’re not planning

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.210
on turning him into a being made of pure sound.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.210
M’Baku

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.330
One of the biggest surprises of Black Panther
came in the form of M’Baku, T’Challa’s

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.640
first challenger to the throne of Wakanda.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.200
Played by Winston Duke, M’Baku is the leader
of the Jabari Tribe, who have withdrawn to

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.090
Wakanda’s mountains, where they live without
dependence on vibranium.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:56.170
His towering physical presence and sense of
humor make him one of the movie’s most memorable

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.170
characters.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.250
But given his comic book roots, it’s kind
of a shock to see M’Baku in a movie at all.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:06.440
Created in 1969, he originally operated under
the rather unfortunate name of “Man-Ape.”

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.230
The idea was to tie him to another jungle
animal in the same way that T’Challa drew

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.980
his powers from the panther, but deeper social
implications made him a pretty awkward supervillain

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:15.980
to have around.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.240
The movie avoids a lot of that awkwardness
by just calling him M’Baku, and toning down

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.370
his costume a little while still keeping nods
to his comic book roots.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.370
They’re small changes, but they make a whole
lot of difference.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.110
As for where he goes from here, the movie
M’Baku does something that he never did

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:33.530
in the comics: he formed an alliance with
T’Challa.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.640
Considering that Winston Duke is already listed
in the cast for Avengers: Infinity War, it’s

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.610
safe to say that this alliance is going to
last… at least for now.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.290
Zuri

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.560
On the screen, Zuri is the spiritual leader
of Wakanda, a priest and advisor to both T’Challa

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.320
and his father, T’Chaka.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.960
The comics tell a different story.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.400
Zuri was one of the Black Panther’s closest
advisors, but his concerns tended to be more

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.400
on the physical side.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.150
He was considered one of Wakanda’s strongest
warriors, and even got into a fistfight with

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.150
Thor.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.240
Of course, since it was Thor, they became
good friends after their throwdown.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.820
Sadly, both versions of Zuri managed to meet
their ultimate fate at the hands of a supervillain.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.370
In the comics, though, it was during an invasion
of Wakanda by Morlun, a Spider-Man villain

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:17.560
who basically eats animal-themed superheroes.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.620
That’s a pretty unlikely fate even for the
Marvel movies, but the real tragedy here is

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.110
that we lost out on Forest Whitaker and Chris
Hemsworth re-enacting Dutch and Dylan’s

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.610
epic handshake from the opening scene of Predator.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:30.610
Ayo

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:35.750
Of all the characters in Black Panther who
deviated from their comic book roots, Ayo,

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.910
played by Florence Kasumba, was the one who
sparked a minor controversy.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:44.630
In the MCU, she’s one of the Dora Milaje,
the order of potential wives-in-training who

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.540
also serve as Wakanda’s security forces,
but not much more.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.220
In the comics, however, she became one of
Marvel’s most prominent LGBTQ characters

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:54.850
shortly after being introduced.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.570
Her lover, Aneka, started out as her combat
trainer, forming a tense relationship that

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.250
developed into much more.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:05.910
Unfortunately, Aneka was convicted of a crime,
and while Ayo pleaded with the reigning queen

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.220
to spare her life, it didn’t work.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.930
Instead, Ayo broke her out and the two became
vigilantes in Wakanda known as the Midnight

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.930
Angels.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.530
While Ayo’s orientation was only hinted
at in scenes that were ultimately cut from

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:21.760
the film, her story did take center stage
when Marvel launched Black Panther: World

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.910
of Wakanda, with the first arc focusing on
Ayo and Aneka’s history.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.240
Nakia

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.120
In the film, Lupita Nyong'o plays Nakia as
T’Challa’s love interest, an ex-girlfriend

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.200
who devoted her life to working in secret
as a spy.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.430
In the end, she steps out of the shadows to
defend Wakanda, and even steps back into her

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.430
very close personal relationship with the
Black Panther.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.220
In the comics… not so much.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:50.550
While Nakia was introduced as one of the original
Dora Milaje in 1998, she didn’t stay as

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.700
one for long.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:56.330
T’Challa’s interest in the Dora Milaje
was purely ceremonial, but Nakia harbored

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:01.180
an infatuation with the superheroic king that
grew to the point of obsession, causing her

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.870
to attempt to murder the Black Panther’s
former girlfriend, Monica Lynne.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:09.830
Nakia was dismissed from his service, and
eventually re-emerged as an enemy named Malice.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.860
It seems unlikely that the MCU’s Nakia will
follow that path, but it’s a lot easier

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.860
to believe that something might drive a wedge
between Nakia and T’Challa to make drama

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.370
in a future film.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.700
After all, since they’re broken up at the
start of the movie, it’s something that’s

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.700
happened to them before.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.380
Thanks for watching!

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:28.090
